+<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>diagnostics-interface</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css?v=20260922"><script src="search-index.js?v=20260922"></script></head><body><nav><h1><a href="README.html">Sonuvi documentation</a></h1><div class="meta">Canonical source: <code>docs/wiki-source/</code></div><details><summary><strong>Start</strong></summary><ul><li><a href="README.html">README</a></li><li><a href="getting-started.html">Getting Started</a></li><li><a href="using-now-playing.html">Using Sonuvi</a></li><li><a href="core-concepts.html">Core Concepts</a></li></ul></details><details><summary><strong>Displays</strong></summary><ul><li><a href="displays.html">Displays</a></li><li><a href="user-interfaces.html">User Interfaces</a></li><li><a href="kiosk-interface.html">Kiosk</a></li><li><a href="visualizer-in-embedded-mode.html">Visualizer</a></li></ul></details><details><summary><strong>Playback and Library</strong></summary><ul><li><a href="playback-features.html">Playback Features</a></li><li><a href="queue-and-playback-model.html">Queue and Playback</a></li><li><a href="queue-wizard-internals.html">Queue Wizard</a></li><li><a href="media-library.html">Media Library</a></li><li><a href="listen-on-device.html">Listen on Device</a></li></ul></details><details><summary><strong>Integrations</strong></summary><ul><li><a href="integrations.html">Integrations</a></li><li><a href="alexa-interface.html">Alexa</a></li><li><a href="youtube-interface.html">YouTube</a></li><li><a href="radio-interface.html">Radio</a></li><li><a href="mabel-integration.html">Mabel</a></li><li><a href="jukebox-priority-model.html">Jukebox Priority</a></li></ul></details><details><summary><strong>Administration</strong></summary><ul><li><a href="configuration-and-administration.html">Configuration and Administration</a></li><li><a href="config-interface.html">Config</a></li><li><a href="diagnostics-interface.html">Diagnostics</a></li><li><a href="deployment-and-ops.html">Deployment and Ops</a></li></ul></details><details><summary><strong>Developer Reference</strong></summary><ul><li><a href="developer-reference.html">Developer Reference</a></li><li><a href="architecture.html">Architecture</a></li><li><a href="api-service-overview.html">API Overview</a></li><li><a href="api-endpoint-catalog.html">API Catalog</a></li><li><a href="source-map.html">Source Map</a></li></ul></details><details><summary><strong>Troubleshooting and Maintenance</strong></summary><ul><li><a href="troubleshooting-and-technical-notes.html">Troubleshooting</a></li><li><a href="playback-issue-triage-runbook.html">Playback Triage</a></li><li><a href="display-surface-troubleshooting.html">Display Triage</a></li><li><a href="documentation-sources-audit.html">Documentation Audit</a></li><li><a href="wiki-operations.html">Wiki Operations</a></li></ul></details></nav><article><h1 id="diagnostics-interface">diagnostics interface</h1><h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2><p>This page documents <code>now-playing/diagnostics.html</code>, the diagnostics and live-inspection console in the <code>now-playing</code> ecosystem.</p><p>This page is intentionally implementation-aware, because <code>diagnostics.html</code> is clearly not just a passive status screen. It acts as a multi-purpose operator console for:</p><ul><li>making direct API requests</li><li>inspecting responses</li><li>testing endpoints with or without <code>x-track-key</code></li><li>viewing live embedded UI surfaces side-by-side</li><li>navigating operational/debug workflows through a browser UI</li></ul><h2 id="why-this-page-matters">Why this page matters</h2><p><code>diagnostics.html</code> appears to be one of the strongest operator-facing truth surfaces in the project.</p><p>It matters because it combines:</p><ul><li>direct endpoint execution</li><li>live embedded views of multiple UI surfaces</li><li>clipboard/curl-oriented debugging helpers</li><li>quick comparison between API behavior and rendered UI behavior</li></ul><p>That makes it extremely valuable when the question is:</p><ul><li>what is the system doing right now?</li><li>what does the API say?</li><li>what does the rendered UI show?</li><li>do those things agree?</li></ul><h2 id="important-files">Important files</h2><p>Primary page:</p><ul><li><code>now-playing/diagnostics.html</code></li></ul><p>Important supporting script:</p><ul><li><code>now-playing/scripts/diagnostics.js</code></li></ul><p>Related pages:</p><ul><li><code>configuration-and-diagnostics-interfaces.md</code></li><li><code>config-interface.md</code></li><li><code>display-surface-troubleshooting.md</code></li><li><code>deployment-and-ops.md</code></li></ul><h2 id="high-level-role">High-level role</h2><p>A good current interpretation is:</p><ul><li><code>diagnostics.html</code> is a browser-based operator/debug console</li><li>it helps bridge raw API behavior and visible UI behavior</li><li>it is one of the most practical pages for side-by-side inspection and debugging</li></ul><p>This means it is not only about diagnostics in the abstract. It is also about comparative observation.</p><h2 id="shell-entry-behavior">Shell / entry behavior</h2><p>Like <code>config.html</code>, <code>diagnostics.html</code> contains shell-redirect behavior.</p><p>Observed logic:</p><ul><li>if <code>standalone=1</code>, remain standalone</li><li>if top-level without standalone, redirect into:</li></ul><p>- <code>app.html?page=diagnostics.html</code></p><ul><li>if embedded in another shell already, do not redirect</li></ul><p>So <code>diagnostics.html</code> is designed for both:</p><ul><li>standalone operator use</li><li>embedded/shell-hosted use inside the broader app</li></ul><h2 id="major-ui-areas-visible-in-the-page">Major UI areas visible in the page</h2><p>Repo-visible structure shows several major diagnostics areas.</p><h2 id="1-request-runner-endpoint-console">1. Request runner / endpoint console</h2><p>This top card includes:</p><ul><li>endpoint filter input</li><li>endpoint selector</li><li>method selector (<code>GET</code> / <code>POST</code>)</li><li>path field</li><li>JSON body field for POST</li><li>run button</li><li>favorite toggle</li><li>copy-as-curl button</li><li>copy-response button</li><li>optional <code>send x-track-key</code> checkbox</li><li>status area</li></ul><p>This is one of the clearest operator/debugging surfaces in the system.</p><h2 id="2-live-embedded-view-cards">2. Live embedded view cards</h2><p>The page includes multiple live embedded-view cards for direct UI inspection, including:</p><ul><li>live now-playing view (<code>index.html</code>)</li><li>live player view (<code>player.html</code>)</li><li>live peppy skin view (<code>peppy.html</code>)</li><li>live controller view (<code>controller.html</code>)</li><li>live kiosk view (<code>kiosk.html</code>)</li></ul><p>Each has controls such as:</p><ul><li>collapse/expand</li><li>reload</li><li>zoom</li><li>open in new tab</li></ul><p>This is a very important design choice.</p><p>It means diagnostics is not only about raw responses - it is also about quickly inspecting multiple rendered surfaces from one operator page.</p><h2 id="important-domaction-centers">Important DOM/action centers</h2><p>Observed important elements include:</p><ul><li><code>#endpointFilter</code></li><li><code>#endpoint</code></li><li><code>#method</code></li><li><code>#path</code></li><li><code>#body</code></li><li><code>#runBtn</code></li><li><code>#favBtn</code></li><li><code>#copyCurlBtn</code></li><li><code>#useTrackKey</code></li><li><code>#copyBtn</code></li><li><code>#status</code></li></ul><p>And for live views:</p><ul><li><code>#liveFrame</code></li><li><code>#playerFrame</code></li><li><code>#peppyFrame</code></li><li><code>#mobileFrame</code></li><li><code>#kioskFrame</code></li><li>their corresponding reload/toggle/zoom controls</li></ul><p>This is enough to treat the page as an active workbench rather than a static inspector.</p><h2 id="diagnostics-script-as-the-logic-center">Diagnostics script as the logic center</h2><p>The main page includes:</p><ul><li><code>scripts/diagnostics.js</code></li></ul><p>That strongly suggests most of the request-running, favorites, frame-management, and interaction logic lives in that script rather than inline.</p><p>So a deeper future pass should likely inspect:</p><ul><li><code>now-playing/scripts/diagnostics.js</code></li></ul><p>in detail.</p><h2 id="request-runner-behavior">Request-runner behavior</h2><p>Based on visible structure, the request runner supports:</p><ul><li>selecting or filtering known endpoints</li><li>choosing method</li><li>editing path manually</li><li>submitting JSON for POST requests</li><li>optionally attaching <code>x-track-key</code></li><li>copying equivalent curl</li><li>favoriting endpoints</li></ul><p>This is architecturally important because it turns diagnostics into a lightweight in-browser API console.</p><p>That can be extremely useful when debugging runtime/admin behavior without leaving the browser UI.</p><h2 id="track-key-aware-diagnostics">Track-key-aware diagnostics</h2><p>The diagnostics page includes:</p><ul><li><code>#useTrackKey</code></li></ul><p>This is a meaningful operator affordance.</p><p>It implies the diagnostics console is designed to test both:</p><ul><li>open/unprotected behavior</li><li>protected/track-key-backed behavior</li></ul><p>That is exactly the sort of thing a real maintenance/debug console should support.</p><h2 id="live-embedded-surface-inspection">Live embedded-surface inspection</h2><p>One of the strongest and most distinctive aspects of <code>diagnostics.html</code> is the set of embedded live views.</p><p>Observed cards include:</p><ul><li><code>index.html</code> at desktop-style viewport size</li><li><code>player.html</code> at 1280×400</li><li><code>peppy.html</code> at 1280×400</li><li><code>controller.html</code> at mobile-ish viewport size</li><li><code>kiosk.html</code></li></ul><p>This means the page supports a workflow like:</p><ol><li>run or inspect an endpoint</li><li>observe the live rendered result in one or more surfaces</li><li>compare whether the API and the UI agree</li><li>reload/reopen affected views quickly</li></ol><p>That is a very powerful debugging pattern.</p><h2 id="relationship-to-display-and-controller-debugging">Relationship to display and controller debugging</h2><p>Because diagnostics embeds multiple live UI surfaces, it sits at the junction of several wiki branches:</p><ul><li>config/diagnostics/admin</li><li>display surfaces</li><li>controller surfaces</li><li>kiosk behavior</li><li>peppy/player render behavior</li></ul><p>That means <code>diagnostics.html</code> may often be a better starting point than a purely conceptual doc when someone is actively debugging a live symptom.</p><h2 id="current-observed-apiconfig-contact">Current observed API/config contact</h2><p>Even from the current light inspection, we can already see:</p><ul><li><code>diagnostics.html</code> fetches runtime config from <code>/config/runtime</code></li><li>it conditionally hides/shows some navigation based on runtime config (for example podcasts visibility)</li><li>it is designed to execute arbitrary configured diagnostic requests through the request-runner UI</li><li><code>scripts/diagnostics.js</code> fetches <code>/config/diagnostics/endpoints</code> to populate a server-backed endpoint list for the request runner</li><li><code>scripts/diagnostics.js</code> also keeps an <code>ENDPOINTS_FALLBACK</code> catalog, which means Diagnostics acts partly as an in-browser endpoint search/catalog surface</li></ul><p>That makes <code>diagnostics.html</code> useful not only for live request execution, but also as an operator-facing API discovery aid.</p><h2 id="architectural-interpretation">Architectural interpretation</h2><p>A good current interpretation is:</p><ul><li><code>diagnostics.html</code> is the browser operator&#x27;s live debugging dashboard</li><li>it combines API-console behavior with live surface monitoring</li><li>it is one of the most direct pages for checking whether data flow and rendered UI are aligned</li></ul><p>That makes it one of the highest-value pages in the diagnostics/admin branch.</p><h2 id="related-branch-pages">Related branch pages</h2><ul><li><code>radio-metadata-eval-interface.md</code></li></ul><p>This is the most direct current wiki page for the dedicated radio metadata QA/evaluation console.</p><h2 id="anatomy-companion-page">Anatomy companion page</h2><ul><li><code>diagnostics-page-anatomy.md</code></li></ul><p>This is the anatomy-style companion page for <code>diagnostics.html</code>. Use it when the task is not just about Diagnostics as a surface, but about a specific region such as the request runner, favorites/endpoint selector, track-key toggle, curl/response helper layer, or embedded live-surface cards.</p><h2 id="relationship-to-other-pages">Relationship to other pages</h2><p>This page should stay linked with:</p><ul><li><code>configuration-and-diagnostics-interfaces.md</code></li><li><code>config-interface.md</code></li><li><code>display-surface-troubleshooting.md</code></li><li><code>deployment-and-ops.md</code></li><li><code>diagnostics-page-anatomy.md</code></li><li>future pages for specific live-surface diagnostics or request-runner details</li></ul><h2 id="things-still-to-verify">Things still to verify</h2><p>Future deeper verification should clarify:</p><ul><li>the exact endpoint catalog and grouping inside <code>scripts/diagnostics.js</code></li><li>how favorites are stored and restored</li><li>how curl-copy generation is implemented</li><li>whether the request runner can safely target all important protected endpoints</li><li>how the live embedded cards are initialized and refreshed</li><li>whether diagnostics includes hidden/advanced features not visible from markup alone</li></ul><p><code>diagnostics-page-anatomy.md</code> matters when the real question is not only “what is <code>diagnostics.html</code>?” but “which region inside <code>diagnostics.html</code> actually owns the thing I need to change?”</p><h2 id="current-status">Current status</h2><p>At the moment, this page already has enough evidence to be treated as one of the project&#x27;s core operator/debugging surfaces.</p><p>It is not just a diagnostics readout. It is a live API console plus multi-surface inspection dashboard, which makes it especially valuable for real troubleshooting work.</p><h2 id="qobuz-audio-info-interpretation">Qobuz Audio Info interpretation</h2><p>The <code>GET /config/moode/audio-info?view=rows</code> surface is source-aware when Qobuz Connect owns the renderer. The Qobuz source format and decoded PCM are reported by the existing Pibuz/moOde <code>get_qbzmeta</code> cache. The hardware output is separate: Sonuvi reads ALSA playback <code>hw_params</code>/<code>status</code> files over the existing read-only moOde SSH path and reports <code>Not measured</code> when no open stream exposes parameters. It does not treat MPD being stopped as proof that the DAC is idle.</p><p>The endpoint labels the moOde <code>audioinfo.php</code> ALSA mode and audio chain as configured moOde/MPD path information, not verified Qobuz signal-path facts. MPD-only volume diagnostics are represented as unavailable for Qobuz, so raw <code>Unknown MPD volume type</code> or ALSA mixer-control errors do not leak into the Qobuz panel. The authenticated mobile audio-info contract forwards the safe <code>audio</code> object and optional diagnostics/provenance without exposing moOde URLs, file paths, PHP session identifiers, or renderer credentials.</p><h2 id="audio-info-diagnostics-contract">Audio Info diagnostics contract</h2><p><code>GET /config/moode/audio-info?view=rows&amp;diagnostics=1</code> reuses the existing bounded, read-only ALSA probe on demand. It reads active playback <code>hw_params</code>/<code>status</code> from <code>/proc/asound</code> over the configured moOde SSH path; it does not open an ALSA device, seize playback, or restart a service. The legacy rows remain available and unchanged when <code>diagnostics=1</code> is omitted.</p><p>The optional <code>diagnostics</code> object is versioned as <code>audio-info.v1</code>. The bearer <code>GET /v1/mobile/audio-info</code> route requests this view when Audio Info is opened and forwards the safe object alongside the existing rows. It is the shared Server/iOS diagnostic contract; native clients do not call the Track-Key <code>/config</code> route directly. Its main fields are:</p><ul><li><code>sourceFormat</code>: source-file or renderer-reported codec, bit depth, rate, and</li></ul><p>channels;</p><ul><li><code>decodedPcm</code>: MPD/renderer-reported decoded format, not a DAC measurement;</li><li><code>configuredOutput</code>: moOde&#x27;s configured ALSA mode and chain, marked</li></ul><p><code>valueType: &quot;configured&quot;</code> and <code>measured: false</code>;</p><ul><li><code>hardwareOutput</code>: the current ALSA playback format, rate, channels, state,</li></ul><p>and device label when procfs exposes them, marked <code>valueType: &quot;hardware-measured&quot;</code>;</p><ul><li><code>processing</code> and <code>volume</code>: configured moOde/MPD state, never represented as</li></ul><p>current hardware measurements;</p><ul><li><code>conversion</code>: deliberately <code>status: &quot;unknown&quot;</code>, because ALSA endpoint</li></ul><p>parameters cannot prove every conversion before the endpoint;</p><ul><li><code>freshness</code>: request-time capture and hardware-measurement timestamps.</li></ul><p>The diagnostics object also has additive normalized fields for metadata accuracy:</p><ul><li><code>sourceFile</code>: container, codec, source bit depth, source sample rate,</li></ul><p>channels, and encoded bitrate. <code>codecStatus: &quot;verified&quot;</code> means the bounded local-file <code>ffprobe</code> supplied the codec; <code>reported</code> means the value came from provider metadata, and <code>unavailable</code> means no codec evidence was available. Lossy codecs report <code>bitDepthStatus: &quot;not-applicable&quot;</code>; decoder precision is never copied into this object. Local files use the existing bounded <code>ffprobe</code> source probe when available, with stat-keyed cache invalidation.</p><ul><li><code>decodedAudio</code>: decoder-reported PCM precision, rate, and channels. Its</li></ul><p><code>pcmBitrateBps</code> is a nominal value calculated from those PCM parameters and is labeled <code>pcmBitrateStatus: &quot;calculated&quot;</code>; an encoded bitrate is not shown as decoded throughput.</p><ul><li><code>output</code>: the same actual ALSA observation as <code>hardwareOutput</code>, with device,</li></ul><p>interface, format, rate, and channels grouped under the output role.</p><p>This distinction is required because moOde&#x27;s <code>audioinfo.php</code> builds its <code>Decoded to</code> string from MPD&#x27;s decoder fields but appends MPD&#x27;s <code>bitrate</code> field to that same string. For AAC, moOde also maps MPD&#x27;s floating-point precision marker (<code>f</code>) to a displayed 24-bit decoder value. Therefore a row such as <code>PCM 24 bit 44.1 kHz, Stereo, 270 kbps</code> means decoder-reported PCM parameters plus an encoded-source bitrate; it does not establish a 24-bit AAC source. The normalized fields keep those meanings separate. When diagnostics are requested and the verified source codec is lossy, the returned legacy <code>Decoded to</code> row omits the trailing encoded bitrate; the compatibility view without diagnostics remains unchanged.</p><p>The mobile response redacts local source paths and network URLs from legacy rows and never forwards the internal moOde URL, Track Key, or PHP session ID.</p><p>The main controller shells disable MPD transport/shuffle/repeat/seek controls while Qobuz owns playback; the server route also rejects those MPD commands with a source-aware unavailable response. No Qobuz remote-control API is implied by the metadata integration.</p><p><em>Last reviewed: 2026-10-20 18:54 America/Chicago</em></p></article><script>(function(){var p=(location.pathname.split("/").pop()||"README.html");document.querySelectorAll("nav a[href]").forEach(function(a){if(a.getAttribute("href")===p){a.classList.add("nav-current");var d=a.closest("details");if(d)d.open=true;}});})();</script><script>(function(){var i=document.getElementById("nav-search");if(!i)return;})();</script></body></html>
