+<!doctype html><html><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"><title>upnp-control-point</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="style.css?v=20260922"><script src="search-index.js?v=20260922"></script></head><body><nav><h1><a href="README.html">Sonuvi documentation</a></h1><div class="meta">Canonical source: <code>docs/wiki-source/</code></div><details><summary><strong>Start</strong></summary><ul><li><a href="README.html">README</a></li><li><a href="getting-started.html">Getting Started</a></li><li><a href="using-now-playing.html">Using Sonuvi</a></li><li><a href="core-concepts.html">Core Concepts</a></li></ul></details><details><summary><strong>Displays</strong></summary><ul><li><a href="displays.html">Displays</a></li><li><a href="user-interfaces.html">User Interfaces</a></li><li><a href="kiosk-interface.html">Kiosk</a></li><li><a href="visualizer-in-embedded-mode.html">Visualizer</a></li></ul></details><details><summary><strong>Playback and Library</strong></summary><ul><li><a href="playback-features.html">Playback Features</a></li><li><a href="queue-and-playback-model.html">Queue and Playback</a></li><li><a href="queue-wizard-internals.html">Queue Wizard</a></li><li><a href="media-library.html">Media Library</a></li><li><a href="listen-on-device.html">Listen on Device</a></li></ul></details><details><summary><strong>Integrations</strong></summary><ul><li><a href="integrations.html">Integrations</a></li><li><a href="alexa-interface.html">Alexa</a></li><li><a href="youtube-interface.html">YouTube</a></li><li><a href="radio-interface.html">Radio</a></li><li><a href="mabel-integration.html">Mabel</a></li><li><a href="jukebox-priority-model.html">Jukebox Priority</a></li></ul></details><details><summary><strong>Administration</strong></summary><ul><li><a href="configuration-and-administration.html">Configuration and Administration</a></li><li><a href="config-interface.html">Config</a></li><li><a href="diagnostics-interface.html">Diagnostics</a></li><li><a href="deployment-and-ops.html">Deployment and Ops</a></li></ul></details><details><summary><strong>Developer Reference</strong></summary><ul><li><a href="developer-reference.html">Developer Reference</a></li><li><a href="architecture.html">Architecture</a></li><li><a href="api-service-overview.html">API Overview</a></li><li><a href="api-endpoint-catalog.html">API Catalog</a></li><li><a href="source-map.html">Source Map</a></li></ul></details><details><summary><strong>Troubleshooting and Maintenance</strong></summary><ul><li><a href="troubleshooting-and-technical-notes.html">Troubleshooting</a></li><li><a href="playback-issue-triage-runbook.html">Playback Triage</a></li><li><a href="display-surface-troubleshooting.html">Display Triage</a></li><li><a href="documentation-sources-audit.html">Documentation Audit</a></li><li><a href="wiki-operations.html">Wiki Operations</a></li></ul></details></nav><article><h1 id="upnpdlna-control-point">UPnP/DLNA control point</h1><h2 id="scope">Scope</h2><p>Sonuvi now contains a generic UPnP/DLNA control-point foundation. Milestone 1 discovers local UPnP devices, reads device and service descriptions, browses compatible <code>ContentDirectory</code> services, normalizes DIDL-Lite metadata, and inspects renderer capabilities. Milestone 2 adds a separately authenticated, single-track AVTransport session with explicit ownership and MPD safeguards. Milestone 3 adds a separately authenticated logical queue above that session; the queue is authoritative for Sonuvi-controlled UPnP playback, while the renderer-owned MPD queue remains an implementation detail.</p><p>These milestone labels describe the implementation sequence; the discovery, session, and logical-queue behaviors described below are present in current main. They are not an unimplemented roadmap.</p><p>This is intentionally separate from:</p><ul><li>the <code>qobuz-connect</code>/Pibuz metadata path;</li><li>direct MPD control;</li><li>native iOS playback; and</li><li>the Sonuvi catalog.</li></ul><p>The UPnP layer does <strong>not</strong> authenticate Qobuz, install Docker, or control Pibuz through its queue/takeover API; the separate authenticated Qobuz Config release route may invoke moOde&#x27;s fixed renderer-disconnect path. UPnP playback mutations remain narrow, Track-Key-authenticated operations over trusted Browse/Search references; there is no raw-URL or generic-SOAP interface.</p><h2 id="implementation">Implementation</h2><p>The control point is implemented in:</p><ul><li><code>src/lib/upnp-control-point.mjs</code> — SSDP discovery, URL policy, bounded HTTP,</li></ul><p>device/service/SCPD parsing, DIDL-Lite normalization, trusted browse-item references, bounded AVTransport actions, and capability inspection.</p><ul><li><code>src/lib/upnp-playback-session.mjs</code> — single-track selection, renderer</li></ul><p>ownership, MPD queue baselines, Qobuz/MPD takeover gates, bounded startup readiness, transport timing diagnostics, and source-neutral Now Playing normalization.</p><ul><li><code>src/lib/upnp-playback-queue.mjs</code> — bounded, in-memory Sonuvi queue state,</li></ul><p>revision-checked mutations, session-generation binding, manual navigation, and confirmed end-of-track advancement.</p><ul><li><code>src/routes/upnp.routes.mjs</code> — authenticated discovery/browse routes plus</li></ul><p>narrow session, transport, and queue routes; there is no generic SOAP endpoint.</p><ul><li><code>controller-upnp.html</code> — the responsive Music Sources browser used by the</li></ul><p>desktop, tablet, and mobile controller shells. It discovers provider-neutral MediaServers, browses/searches their ContentDirectory, and presents the separate Sonuvi UPnP queue.</p><ul><li><code>scripts/upnp-now-playing.js</code> — a source-aware bridge for the existing Now</li></ul><p>Playing controls. It routes next/previous/pause/resume/play actions to the authenticated UPnP queue only while <code>/now-playing</code> identifies <code>upnp-sonuvi</code>; it never becomes a generic SOAP or URL control surface.</p><ul><li><code>test/upnp-control-point.test.mjs</code> and <code>test/upnp-playback-session.test.mjs</code></li></ul><p>— fixture-driven parser, security, SOAP, discovery, browse, renderer, allowlist, ownership, queue-protection, lifecycle, and no-raw-URL coverage.</p><ul><li><code>test/upnp-playback-queue.test.mjs</code> — queue ordering, revision conflicts,</li></ul><p>lifecycle, navigation, duplicate-event suppression, resource-URL omission, and external-takeover fixtures.</p><p>The server creates one in-memory <code>UpnpControlPoint</code>. Device records and SCPD descriptions are cached only for the process lifetime. The active logical queue is also memory-only by design: it retains opaque media-server/item references and metadata, but never persists signed stream/resource URLs. A restart therefore drops the queue and any Sonuvi UPnP ownership without sending a renderer command.</p><h2 id="interfaces">Interfaces</h2><p>All routes use the existing Sonuvi Track Key authorization boundary. The server never accepts a device URL from a browser. A browser supplies only a registered device ID and bounded ContentDirectory parameters.</p><table><thead><tr><th>Route</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>GET /upnp/devices</code></td><td>Run bounded SSDP discovery and return registered devices. Use <code>refresh=1</code> for an explicit refresh.</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /upnp/devices/:deviceId</code></td><td>Return a registered device and its dynamically resolved services/actions.</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /upnp/media-servers/:deviceId/capabilities</code></td><td>Inspect ContentDirectory actions and read-only search/sort capabilities.</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /upnp/media-servers/:deviceId/browse</code></td><td>Read <code>Browse</code> results and normalize DIDL-Lite containers/items/resources.</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /upnp/media-servers/:deviceId/search</code></td><td>Use <code>Search</code> only when the service advertises it.</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /upnp/media-items/:selectionRef/artwork</code></td><td>Read bounded artwork through a trusted opaque item reference; browsers never fetch the advertised LAN URL directly.</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /upnp/renderers/:deviceId/capabilities</code></td><td>Inspect AVTransport, RenderingControl, and ConnectionManager SCPDs plus read-only renderer state/protocol information.</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /upnp/preflight?rendererId=...</code></td><td>Read Qobuz/MPD/renderer ownership state and capture the MPD queue baseline. It never mutates playback.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /upnp/sessions</code></td><td>Select a recent opaque Browse/Search item reference and the approved renderer. It validates the item and captures a baseline but does not send AVTransport.</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /upnp/sessions/:sessionId</code></td><td>Read session/transport state and detect ownership loss.</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /upnp/sessions/:sessionId/transport</code></td><td>Read-only transport-state alias.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /upnp/sessions/:sessionId/load</code></td><td>Send generated DIDL-Lite and the trusted resource through <code>SetAVTransportURI</code>. This is a playback mutation and requires separate operator approval.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /upnp/sessions/:sessionId/play</code></td><td>Send AVTransport <code>Play</code> after ownership validation.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /upnp/sessions/:sessionId/pause</code></td><td>Send AVTransport <code>Pause</code> after ownership validation.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /upnp/sessions/:sessionId/stop</code></td><td>Send AVTransport <code>Stop</code> after ownership validation and release ownership.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /upnp/queues</code></td><td>Create the bounded logical queue for one approved renderer, optionally with ordered opaque item references.</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /upnp/queue</code></td><td>Read the current process-local queue without requiring the browser to know its queue ID first. This is read-only and returns an empty snapshot when no queue exists.</td></tr><tr><td><code>GET /upnp/queues/:queueId</code></td><td>Read queue order, revision, current item, session generation, and confirmed renderer state.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /upnp/queues/:queueId/items</code></td><td>Append one or more trusted Browse/Search item references in order.</td></tr><tr><td><code>DELETE /upnp/queues/:queueId/items/:itemId</code></td><td>Remove an inactive queue item.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /upnp/queues/:queueId/clear</code></td><td>Stop the Sonuvi-owned session, then clear the logical queue.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /upnp/queues/:queueId/reorder</code></td><td>Move an item by queue item ID while preserving the current item identity.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /upnp/queues/:queueId/play</code></td><td>Play the selected item through the approved renderer.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /upnp/queues/:queueId/resume</code></td><td>Resume the current paused item.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /upnp/queues/:queueId/pause</code></td><td>Pause the current Sonuvi-owned item.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /upnp/queues/:queueId/stop</code></td><td>Stop the current item and release Sonuvi ownership.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /upnp/queues/:queueId/next</code></td><td>Stop the current item and play the next queue item when available.</td></tr><tr><td><code>POST /upnp/queues/:queueId/previous</code></td><td>Stop the current item and play the previous queue item when available.</td></tr></tbody></table><p>The API preserves the provider-neutral shape needed later by a Sonuvi control point: device identity, UDN, object/container IDs, opaque selection references, track metadata, artwork references, resource URIs, duration, MIME/protocol information, and advertised audio fields such as sample rate and bit depth. A transport mutation accepts only an opaque selection reference returned by the server&#x27;s recent Browse/Search state; it never accepts a raw URL from a browser.</p><h2 id="native-mobile-upnp-contract">Native mobile UPnP contract</h2><p><code>src/routes/mobile-upnp.routes.mjs</code> exposes the same Server-owned UPnP engine through a bearer-safe <code>/v1/mobile/upnp/<em></code> bridge. Native iPhone/iPad/CarPlay clients must use this family rather than sending the Track Key to <code>/upnp/</em></code>.</p><p>The bridge covers:</p><ul><li>discovery and safe device details;</li><li>media-server capabilities, browse/search, and mediated item artwork;</li><li>renderer capabilities and read-only transport;</li><li>preflight and session/transport reads; and</li><li>logical queue create/read/add/remove/clear/reorder, explicit takeover, and</li></ul><p>play/resume/pause/stop/next/previous mutations.</p><p>Mobile responses are sanitized: they expose opaque device, selection, queue, revision, session, and generation values plus allowlisted capabilities and last-confirmed renderer state. They do not expose device-advertised LAN URLs, MPD paths/IDs, SOAP, or signed resource URLs.</p><p>Queue mutations require the current integer <code>revision</code>. Transport remains bound to the renderer/session generation. The dedicated <code>POST /v1/mobile/upnp/queues/:queueId/takeover</code> route requires literal <code>confirm: true</code> and the current <code>sessionGeneration</code>; an explicit <code>takeover: true</code> on Play Now is the compatibility equivalent. Ordinary Play does not silently take over active MPD, Qobuz, or uncertain renderer state.</p><p>This mobile bridge does not create a second queue or a second UPnP engine. The Server remains the authority for the logical Sonuvi queue and playback ownership; native iOS is a caller of that contract.</p><h2 id="web-controller-integration">Web controller integration</h2><p>The three controller shells expose one <code>Music Sources</code> navigation entry:</p><ul><li><code>controller.html</code> for desktop/kiosk layouts;</li><li><code>controller-tablet.html</code> for tablet and right-pane layouts; and</li><li><code>controller-mobile.html</code> for phone layouts.</li></ul><p>All three route to the same responsive <code>controller-upnp.html</code> page. The page keeps the existing controller theme/profile conventions, uses stable device IDs/UDNs for source identity, and does not hard-code Qobuz as a special source. An eventual Qobuz UPnP ContentDirectory therefore appears as an ordinary MediaServer.</p><p>The page only shows a renderer after the authenticated server capability probe accepts it as the approved moOde renderer. It displays the logical UPnP queue separately from the MPD Live Queue and sends queue mutations with the current revision. A <code>409</code> revision or ownership conflict refreshes authoritative state and is not retried destructively. Browse/Search item titles, metadata, and artwork are rendered as untrusted data; artwork is fetched through the server-side opaque-reference proxy with bounded local-host validation.</p><p>Page initialization performs only discovery, ContentDirectory reads, read-only renderer inspection, and current-queue reads. It does not create a queue, select a track, or issue AVTransport commands. Playback remains an explicit user action and is still guarded by the server&#x27;s ownership and MPD/ Qobuz gates.</p><h2 id="qobuz-account-status-and-unauthenticated-browsing">Qobuz account status and unauthenticated browsing</h2><p>The separate Qobuz account-status surface (not the Qobuz Connect transport surface) adds the authenticated read-only route <code>GET /config/qobuz/account/status</code>. It probes discovered MediaServer roots and identifies the Qobuz provider only when the root contains its <code>0$qobuz$</code> namespace. That proves MediaServer discovery, not account login; the response therefore reports <code>not_connected</code> with authentication <code>not_verified</code> until an approved authorization mechanism exists. The response contains no Qobuz password, token, application material, authorization code, cache path, or signed stream URL.</p><p>The Config page presents this state in a native-looking Qobuz Account card with a disabled <code>Connect Qobuz</code> action. There is no browser password form and no active OAuth callback. Future connect/status/cancel/disconnect endpoints remain design-only and must keep credentials on moOde.</p><p>Browse and Search requests under the Qobuz object namespace receive the sanitized <code>QOBUZ_AUTH_REQUIRED</code> error only when the bounded UPnP request times out. Other media-server timeouts remain <code>UPNP_TIMEOUT</code>, so a generic network failure is not mislabeled as an authentication failure. The web controller clears its loading state within its client bound and links the operator to Config for Qobuz status.</p><p>The installed moOde Qobuz plugin has a tracked local security override before any account test: callback request/code/token values are not logged, the callback reports success only after a user ID and token are returned, and the Qobuz cache is protected by directory mode <code>0700</code> plus an upmpdcli systemd <code>UMask=0077</code>. The patch and offline/live checks are maintained under <code>ops/moode-overrides/</code>. This does not make the undocumented Qobuz authentication protocol officially authorized; package upgrades require a fresh source review and a clean patch application.</p><h2 id="logical-queue-and-ownership">Logical queue and ownership</h2><p>The queue manager stores a bounded ordered list of generated queue item IDs. Each item retains the media-server ID, ContentDirectory item ID, opaque selection reference, metadata, artwork reference, duration, and advertised resource format. It deliberately omits the resource URI: Qobuz-like servers may return signed or short-lived URLs, so the existing playback session re-resolves and validates the current resource during <code>select</code>/<code>load</code>.</p><p>Every mutation after queue creation requires the current integer <code>revision</code>. The server returns <code>UPNP_QUEUE_REVISION_REQUIRED</code> for a missing revision and <code>UPNP_QUEUE_REVISION_CONFLICT</code> with the current revision for a stale command. The queue also binds playback to its renderer ID, session ID, and session generation. A session owned by another UPnP controller, an active Qobuz Connect session, an active/paused ordinary MPD session, or uncertain renderer state rejects takeover.</p><p>The renderer&#x27;s MPD queue is not the Sonuvi queue. <code>SetAVTransportURI</code> may replace the renderer&#x27;s MPD playlist, as observed with moOde/upmpdcli; Sonuvi does not snapshot or silently restore that implementation queue. Queue advancement occurs only after a successful renderer-state observation confirms the prior item was playing, is now stopped, and has reached its reported duration. Poll failures, stale sessions, and external URI changes do not advance the queue. A process restart clears the memory-only logical queue.</p><p>The queue uses <code>upnp-sonuvi</code> as its source identity and shares the existing session manager&#x27;s MPD-mutation guard and MPD-history suppression. It does not add a second history or Last.fm event for the MPD-backed renderer playback.</p><h2 id="discovery-and-trust-boundary">Discovery and trust boundary</h2><p>Discovery sends SSDP <code>M-SEARCH</code> only for MediaServer and MediaRenderer device types over eligible private IPv4 interfaces. Responses are deduplicated by advertised location, then descriptions are fetched with:</p><ul><li>explicit HTTP/HTTPS and no URL credentials;</li><li>local/private IP or local naming-domain validation;</li><li>no redirect following;</li><li>bounded response bodies and request bodies;</li><li>request timeouts that cover response-body reads; and</li><li>DTD/entity rejection with bounded XML parsing.</li></ul><p>Device descriptions may advertise relative service URLs. Sonuvi resolves them against the description URL or <code>URLBase</code>, then applies the same local URL policy before fetching SCPDs or issuing a read-only SOAP request.</p><p>Resource URLs are retained as authenticated local API data for the bounded session flow. Before <code>SetAVTransportURI</code>, Sonuvi validates the resource as a trusted local URL, requires it to originate from the selected media server, matches its protocol/MIME data against the approved renderer&#x27;s <code>GetProtocolInfo</code> sink, and generates DIDL-Lite metadata itself. Sonuvi does not expose a generic URL-fetch or generic SOAP route.</p><h2 id="contentdirectory-model">ContentDirectory model</h2><p><code>Browse</code> supports <code>BrowseMetadata</code> and <code>BrowseDirectChildren</code>, bounded pagination, and a bounded filter/sort string. <code>Search</code> is available only when the discovered SCPD advertises the action. Sonuvi does not invent search capabilities or assume that all servers expose artist, album, favorites, or playlist categories.</p><p>DIDL-Lite normalization preserves:</p><ul><li>item/container kind and stable server IDs;</li><li>parent and reference IDs;</li><li>title, creator, artist(s), album artist, album, and track number;</li><li>UPnP class and artwork URI;</li><li>duration and parsed duration seconds;</li><li>resource URI, protocolInfo, MIME type, DLNA/additional protocol data; and</li><li>bounded audio resource fields such as sample frequency, bits per sample,</li></ul><p>channels, bitrate, size, and resolution.</p><p>Favorites and playlist writes are not implemented. No account-library mutation is implied by a server advertising a ContentDirectory action.</p><h2 id="renderer-inspection">Renderer inspection</h2><p>Renderer inspection loads service descriptions dynamically and reports the advertised action set rather than assuming that every renderer supports seek, volume, queue replacement, gapless playback, or a particular FLAC resolution.</p><p>The read-only probes are limited to:</p><ul><li>ConnectionManager <code>GetProtocolInfo</code>;</li><li>AVTransport <code>GetTransportInfo</code>;</li><li>AVTransport <code>GetPositionInfo</code>;</li><li>AVTransport <code>GetMediaInfo</code>; and</li><li>AVTransport <code>GetCurrentTransportActions</code>.</li></ul><p>URI query strings are removed from the returned current/next URI diagnostics, and the API reports whether current metadata exists without returning raw metadata that could contain a session URL. No renderer ownership is changed.</p><h3 id="single-track-startup-readiness">Single-track startup readiness</h3><p>For a Sonuvi-owned track, the session waits for <code>SetAVTransportURI</code> to return, then polls <code>GetMediaInfo</code> until the renderer reports the expected resource URI. When available, <code>GetTransportInfo</code> is polled as well; <code>TRANSITIONING</code> delays <code>Play</code>, while a stable state or an unavailable state probe allows startup to continue once the expected URI is confirmed. <code>GetPositionInfo</code> is supplementary and records whether the renderer has exposed a duration. A bounded timeout or an unsupported/incomplete <code>GetMediaInfo</code> response falls back to the existing ownership check and <code>Play</code> flow. A short settle interval follows confirmed readiness only.</p><p>If a <code>Play</code> SOAP response is ambiguous, the session re-reads renderer state before responding. An already active renderer is adopted without another <code>Play</code>; if no active state can be confirmed, Sonuvi refuses to resend because <code>STOPPED</code> could also mean a very short track already finished. A definite SOAP fault remains retryable. Startup polling is session-bound, and late observations from a replaced session are ignored. This startup handshake does not change the separate <code>SetNextAVTransportURI</code> gapless preparation path.</p><p>The renderer-compatible DIDL resource URI is fetched directly by the renderer from the registered MediaServer. Sonuvi validates the resource origin and matches its advertised <code>protocolInfo</code>/MIME type to the renderer sink, then passes it to <code>SetAVTransportURI</code>; it does not proxy the media response. The server therefore cannot observe the Denon&#x27;s first media GET or verify that server&#x27;s redirect chain, HTTP response MIME type, <code>Content-Length</code>, byte-range responses, or transcoding behavior. The normalized DIDL <code>size</code> and MIME fields are advertised metadata, not measurements of the media HTTP response. The first renderer request is explicitly logged as unobservable, without recording the resource URL. A packet capture or Jellyfin/MediaServer access log is still needed to distinguish renderer startup delay from a slow or unsuitable media response. No Jellyfin-specific media or transcoding route is present in this UPnP playback path.</p><p>Timing logs include Set URI send/completion, URI readiness/fallback, Play send/completion or ambiguous failure, the first observed post-Play transport state change, and the first non-zero position. They include elapsed durations and a per-start trace ID, but never the resource URI or its query token.</p><h2 id="relationship-to-moode-and-current-playback">Relationship to moOde and current playback</h2><p>The implemented path is:</p><pre><code>UPnP MediaServer ContentDirectory
+        | Browse/Search
+        v
+Sonuvi control point and queue
+        | guarded AVTransport/session control
+        v
+moOde UPnP renderer -&gt; MPD -&gt; ALSA -&gt; CAVALLI DAC</code></pre><p>The current Server path uses the existing moOde renderer by discovered UDN/ device ID and the expected moOde host; it does not select a renderer by friendly name. It rejects selection while Qobuz Connect is active/stale, MPD is playing/paused, or the renderer is loaded/active/uncertain. It captures the MPD queue hash and never restores a queue automatically. While a Sonuvi UPnP session owns the renderer, the normal MPD/queue mutation surfaces are rejected and the listening-history observer is suppressed so the same MPD-backed playback is not recorded a second time. External renderer URI changes invalidate the session. The logical queue wraps this session as described above; Qobuz Connect remains a separate source and Sonuvi never routes queue commands through Pibuz.</p><h2 id="configuration-and-operations">Configuration and operations</h2><p>Optional environment controls are:</p><ul><li><code>UPNP_DISCOVERY_INTERFACES</code> — comma-separated interface names;</li><li><code>UPNP_DISCOVERY_TIMEOUT_MS</code> — SSDP response window;</li><li><code>UPNP_HTTP_TIMEOUT_MS</code> — bounded device/SCPD/SOAP request timeout;</li><li><code>UPNP_MAX_XML_BYTES</code> — device/SCPD body limit;</li><li><code>UPNP_MAX_SOAP_BYTES</code> — SOAP response body limit; and</li><li><code>UPNP_DISCOVERY_COOLDOWN_MS</code> — minimum cached-discovery interval; and</li><li><code>UPNP_STARTUP_URI_READY_TIMEOUT_MS</code> — maximum URI-readiness polling window</li></ul><p>(default 3000 ms);</p><ul><li><code>UPNP_STARTUP_POLL_INTERVAL_MS</code> — interval between readiness probes</li></ul><p>(default 150 ms); and</p><ul><li><code>UPNP_STARTUP_SETTLE_MS</code> — short delay after confirmed URI readiness</li></ul><p>(default 150 ms); and</p><ul><li><code>UPNP_MOODE_RENDERER_HOST</code> — approved renderer discovery host (defaults to</li></ul><p><code>10.0.0.254</code>).</p><p>The service is deployed as part of Sonuvi only. No Docker, package, moOde, Pibuz, firewall, or AirPlay change is required. Rollback consists of removing the new UPnP module/routes and restoring the prior server entrypoint, followed by restarting only <code>now-playing.service</code>.</p><h2 id="verification-status">Verification status</h2><p>The automated suite includes the existing Sonuvi tests plus UPnP fixtures for SSDP, relative URL resolution, malformed/unsafe XML, response limits, SOAP faults, DIDL-Lite metadata, ContentDirectory browsing, renderer capability probes, bounded AVTransport actions, selection/session ownership, queue protection, lifecycle failures, and a no-playback-mutation assertion. Startup-session fixtures also cover expected URI readiness before <code>Play</code>, delayed renderer readiness, timeout fallback, incomplete state probes, ambiguous-Play duplicate prevention, stale observations, and timing-log URL redaction.</p><p>The live verification records the devices and capabilities found on the trusted LAN and captures a read-only MPD queue baseline. It must not be interpreted as authorization to begin playback. Queue deployment and read-only route checks do not send <code>SetAVTransportURI</code>, <code>Play</code>, <code>Pause</code>, or <code>Stop</code>; a multi-track queue acceptance test remains separately approved and must be coordinated step by step.</p><p>Last reviewed: 2026-10-20 18:54 America/Chicago.</p></article><script>(function(){var p=(location.pathname.split("/").pop()||"README.html");document.querySelectorAll("nav a[href]").forEach(function(a){if(a.getAttribute("href")===p){a.classList.add("nav-current");var d=a.closest("details");if(d)d.open=true;}});})();</script><script>(function(){var i=document.getElementById("nav-search");if(!i)return;})();</script></body></html>
